<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Healthcare hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Cause comes first</strong><span>Low intake, illness, medications and organ function can create very different fluid problems.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Severity changes the plan</strong><span>Oral fluids may help in some situations. Severe illness or unsafe swallowing requires medical assessment.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Use products as labelled</strong><span>A possible healthcare use is not automatically an authorized clinical indication.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Clinical framing</p>
    <h2>“Hydration” is not one diagnosis</h2>
    <p>Fluid balance can change because of poor intake, gastrointestinal losses, fever, medication, kidney or heart disease and many other factors. The correct response depends on the cause and the person’s overall condition.</p>
    <p>General hydration support is different from diagnosing or treating dehydration, electrolyte disorders or disease.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Application areas</p>
  <h2>Where hydration questions come up</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Illness & fluid loss</h3><p>Vomiting, diarrhoea, fever and poor intake can increase losses. Mild support, therapeutic oral rehydration solution and medical treatment are not interchangeable.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Recovery & rehabilitation</h3><p>Mobility, appetite, medications, swallowing and access to drinks can affect hydration during recovery.</p><a href="/pages/recovery-rehabilitation">Recovery & Rehabilitation →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Active aging</h3><p>Thirst, kidney function, mobility, medications and dependence on others can all affect hydration in older adults.</p><a href="/pages/active-aging">Active Aging →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Clinician-guided hydration</h3><p>Some conditions require individualized fluid, sodium, potassium or volume targets rather than a standard consumer recommendation.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/8413300/pexels-photo-8413300.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Older adult discussing health concerns with a medical professional in a clinic" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Older adults</p>
    <h2>Risk can rise, but generic advice is not enough</h2>
    <p>Older adults may have reduced thirst, lower total-body water reserves, mobility barriers, swallowing difficulty or medication-related fluid concerns. Signs of dehydration can also be nonspecific.</p>
    <p>That makes hydration important, but “drink more” can be inappropriate for people with fluid restrictions, heart failure, advanced kidney disease or other individualized plans.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Oral rehydration</p>
    <h2>Consumer hydration and therapeutic ORS are different</h2>
    <p>World Health Organization reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts are a defined therapeutic formulation used for diarrhoeal dehydration. An electrolyte drink may share ingredients without being equivalent in formulation, intended use or evidence.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate’s current product should be used according to its authorized label. Additional clinical questions belong in appropriately designed research.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Do not conflate</span>
    <h3>Electrolyte drink ≠ therapeutic ORS.</h3>
    <p>Clinical equivalence requires the relevant formulation, intended use and evidence.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">When self-directed drinking is not enough</p>
  <h2>Situations that need clinical judgment</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Fluid or sodium restrictions</h3><p>Heart, kidney, liver or endocrine conditions can change safe fluid and electrolyte targets.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Unsafe swallowing or altered consciousness</h3><p>People who cannot drink safely, are significantly confused or cannot keep fluids down need assessment rather than repeated self-treatment.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Severe or persistent losses</h3><p>Ongoing losses, very low intake, marked weakness or other signs of significant illness require evaluation of the cause and severity.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context">
